1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,110
A notice lands on WhatsApp, as a PDF.

2
00:00:04,410 --> 00:00:06,560
Is it what the board actually published?

3
00:00:08,210 --> 00:00:11,240
Notice pages change quietly. Files get withdrawn.

4
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:16,450
And public archives can be blocked. India
blocked the Wayback Machine in 2017.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,550
Sakshi is a witness.

6
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:24,640
It records which exact file a
notice URL served, and when.

7
00:00:26,990 --> 00:00:30,860
Every file has a fingerprint: its SHA-256 hash.

8
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:34,270
Move one deadline, and the
fingerprint changes completely.

9
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:43,110
A witness fetches each notice, hashes it, and
writes a read-only observation to Arkiv.

10
00:00:43,410 --> 00:00:45,960
Seen. Changed. Or gone.

11
00:00:49,410 --> 00:00:53,360
Each record is attributed to the wallet that
created it, and placed in a block.

12
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:56,730
Nobody, including us, can quietly edit it.

13
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:03,470
To check a PDF, your browser computes
its fingerprint locally.

14
00:01:03,770 --> 00:01:08,240
Then it asks Arkiv who saw that exact
file. Nothing is uploaded.

15
00:01:13,190 --> 00:01:16,820
There are four answers. Served. Replaced. Removed.

16
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:20,470
Or no matching record, which is
never proof of a fake.

17
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:25,730
You choose which witnesses to trust.

18
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:32,940
Notice one was seen by two witness identities. Untrust one, and
it counts one. Untrust both, and there is no match.

19
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:39,110
Both identities are run by our team, so they are
distinct wallets, not independent organisations.

20
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:47,750
It is live on Arkiv Tiramisu, with six real
transactions and two witness wallets.

21
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:51,680
And the same check runs from the command
line, without our website.

22
00:01:56,030 --> 00:01:58,660
Sakshi. The truth leaves a trace.
